WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.110
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:09.810
Today we are going to see that virtual bones
make everything better.

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:15.299
This new paper is about setting up bones and
joints for our virtual characters to be able

00:00:15.299 --> 00:00:18.420
to compute deformations.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:24.240
Deformations are at the heart of computer
animation, look, all of these sequences require

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:29.820
a carefully designed technique that can move
these joints around and simulate its effect

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.870
on the entirety of the body.

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:35.200
Things move around, stretch, and bulge.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.379
But, there is a problem.

00:00:38.379 --> 00:00:40.129
What’s the problem?

00:00:40.129 --> 00:00:45.429
Well, even with state of the art deformation
techniques, sometimes, this happens.

00:00:45.429 --> 00:00:47.760
Did you catch it?

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.359
There is the problem, look!

00:00:50.359 --> 00:00:54.840
The hip region unfortunately bulges inwards.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.309
Is this specific to this technique?

00:00:57.309 --> 00:01:02.190
No-no, not in the slightest - pretty much
all of the previous techniques showcase that

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:03.289
to some effect.

00:01:03.289 --> 00:01:07.580
This is perhaps our most intense case of this
inward bulging.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:11.360
So, let’s have a taste of the new method.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.870
How does it deal with a case like this?

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:16.460
Perfectly, that’s how.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:17.590
Loving it.

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:23.400
Now, hold on to your papers, because it works
by creating something that the authors refer

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.730
to as virtual bones.

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:29.180
Let’s look under the hood and locate them.

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:31.400
There they are!

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:34.750
These red dots showcase these virtual bones.

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:40.310
We can set them up as a parameter and the
algorithm distributes them automatically.

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:49.240
Here, we have a 100 of them, but we can go
to 200, or, if we so desire, we can request

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:51.690
even 500 of them.

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:55.040
So, what difference does this make?

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.280
With a 100 virtual bones.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:59.400
Let’s see…yes.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:05.330
Here you see that the cooler colors like blue
showcase the regions that are deforming accurately,

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:10.810
and the warmer colors, for instance, red showcases
the problematic regions where the technique

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:12.180
did not perform well.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:18.319
The red part means that these deformations
can be off by about 2 centimeters, or about

00:02:18.319 --> 00:02:20.409
three quarters of an inch.

00:02:20.409 --> 00:02:26.150
I would say that is great news, because even
even with only a 100 virtual bones, we get

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:28.180
an acceptable animation.

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:34.049
However, the technique is still somewhat inaccurate
around the knee and the hips.

00:02:34.049 --> 00:02:39.909
However, if you are one of our really precise
Fellow Scholars, and feel that even that tiny

00:02:39.909 --> 00:02:48.780
mismatch is too much, we can raise the number
of virtual bones to 500, and, let’s see…there

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:49.959
we go!

00:02:49.959 --> 00:02:55.459
Still some imperfections around the knees,
but, the rest is accurate to a small fraction

00:02:55.459 --> 00:02:57.560
of an inch.

00:02:57.560 --> 00:02:58.560
Excellent.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:04.129
The hips and knees seem to be a common theme,
look, they show up in this example too.

00:03:04.129 --> 00:03:10.959
And, as in the previous case, even the 100
virtual bone animation is acceptable, and

00:03:10.959 --> 00:03:15.760
most of the problems can be remedied by adding
500 of them.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:17.750
Still some issues around the elbows.

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:25.500
So far, we have looked at the new solution,
and marked the good and bad regions with heatmaps.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:31.790
So now, how about looking at the reference
footage and the new technique side by side.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:33.120
Why?

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:38.819
Because we’ll find out whether it is just
good at fooling the human eye, or does it

00:03:38.819 --> 00:03:40.409
really match up?

00:03:40.409 --> 00:03:42.790
Let’s have a look together.

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:46.540
This is linear blend skinning, a state of
the art method.

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:49.799
For now, we can accept this as a reference.

00:03:49.799 --> 00:03:56.389
Note that setting this up is expensive both
in terms of computation, and it also requires

00:03:56.389 --> 00:04:00.939
a skilled artist to place these helper joints
correctly.

00:04:00.939 --> 00:04:02.569
This looks great.

00:04:02.569 --> 00:04:07.499
So how does the new method with the virtual
bones look under the hood?

00:04:07.499 --> 00:04:12.829
These correspond to those, so, why do all
this?

00:04:12.829 --> 00:04:17.030
Because the new method can be computed much,
much cheaper.

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:20.180
So let’s see what the results look like!

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:21.470
Mmmm!

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:22.759
Yeah!

00:04:22.759 --> 00:04:24.940
Very close to the reference results.

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:26.150
Absolutely amazing.

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:32.419
Now, let’s run a torture test that would
make any computer graphics researcher blush.

00:04:32.419 --> 00:04:34.069
Oh my.

00:04:34.069 --> 00:04:38.919
There are so many characters here animated
at the same time.

00:04:38.919 --> 00:04:42.919
So how long do we have to wait for these accurate
simulations?

00:04:42.919 --> 00:04:44.600
Minutes to hours?

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:47.310
Let me know your guess in the comments below.

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:49.370
I’ll wait.

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:50.370
Thank you!

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:56.729
Now, hold on to your papers because all this
takes about 5 milliseconds per frame.

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:58.800
5 milliseconds!

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:04.569
This seems well over a hundred characters
rocking out, and the new technique doesn’t

00:05:04.569 --> 00:05:06.290
even break a sweat.

00:05:06.290 --> 00:05:11.810
So, I hope that with this, computer animations
are going to become a lot more realistic in

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:13.750
the near future.

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:15.360
What a time to be alive!

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:18.900
Also, make sure to have a look at the paper
in the video description.

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:23.410
I loved the beautiful mathematics, and the
clarity in there.

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:28.590
It clearly states the contributions in a bulleted
list, which is a more and more common occurrence,

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:30.710
that’s good, but look!

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:37.470
It even provides an image of these contributions
right there, making it even clearer to the

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:38.470
reader.

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:43.880
Generally, details like this show that the
authors went out of their way and spent a

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:48.220
great deal of extra time writing a crystal-clear
paper.

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:54.091
It takes much, much more time than many may
imagine, so, I would like to send big thank

00:05:54.091 --> 00:05:56.330
you to the authors for that.

00:05:56.330 --> 00:06:47.389
Way 

00:06:47.389 --> 00:06:57.530
to go!

00:06:57.530 --> 00:07:00.869
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

